Consider the following discrete-time activation process on a random directed graph. Let $n \ge 1$ and form a random digraph $D(n,\pi)$ on vertex set $\{1,\dots,n\}$ by independently including each ordered pair $(i,j)$ with $i \ne j$ as a directed edge with probability $\pi = \pi(n) = c/n$, where $c>0$ is a fixed constant. Each vertex $i$ has a threshold $\mathrm{th}_i = 1$ and an initial activation parameter $p_i = 1$, interpreted as follows: at time $t=0$ a single vertex chosen uniformly at random is active, and at each subsequent step every inactive vertex $i$ becomes active as soon as it has received at least $\mathrm{th}_i = 1$ directed edge from a currently active vertex. Let $A_n(c)$ denote the (random) total number of vertices that are eventually active.

It is known that there exists a critical constant $c_{\mathrm{crit}} \in [1,2]$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $c < c_{\mathrm{crit}}$, then $A_n(c)/n \to 0$ in probability as $n\to\infty$;
\item if $c > c_{\mathrm{crit}}$, then $\liminf_{n\to\infty} \mathbb{E}[A_n(c)]/n > 0$.
\end{itemize}

Prove that $c_{\mathrm{crit}} = 1$.

